\section*{Abstract}
We present a closed-loop regenerative control architecture---the \emph{Sovereign Regenerative Engine}---designed to govern biological regeneration across molecular, cellular, and tissue scales. The system enforces strict invariants on cell-cycle dynamics, maintains topological and geometric integrity of tissue manifolds, and prevents neoplastic drift via recursion-safe mitosis control. We formalize the timing, invariant, closure, and recursion subsystems and show how they integrate into a single sovereign kernel loop.

\section{System Overview}
Let $\Omega$ denote a multi-domain bio-mechanical test manifold representing the target biological substrate. The Sovereign Regenerative Engine operates over discrete time steps $\Delta t$ and maintains a global invariant clamp:
\[
\Delta \mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}} \equiv 0,
\]
where $\mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}}$ encodes cell-cycle invariants and topological constraints.

At each time step, the engine executes a four-phase cycle:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Sense \& Ingest
  \item Compute \& Validate
  \item Decide \& Act
  \item Stabilize \& Audit
\end{enumerate}

\section{Regeneration Timing Model $T_{\text{reg}}$}
We define a stress tensor field $\mathcal{E}_{\text{stress}}(t)$ over $\Omega$ and a regeneration timing function
\[
T_{\text{reg}} = \tau_0 \cdot f\big(\mathcal{E}_{\text{stress}}(t)\big),
\]
where $\tau_0$ is a baseline refractory period and $f$ is a monotone function that dilates or contracts the regeneration window based on stress magnitude.

A damping coefficient $\sigma_{\text{threshold}}$ modulates activation:
\[
\sigma_{\text{threshold}} = g\big(\mathcal{E}_{\text{stress}}(t)\big),
\]
with $g$ chosen such that high stress induces stronger damping, preventing runaway regenerative pulses.

\section{Cell-Cycle Invariant System $\mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}}$}
Let $n$ denote the mitosis generation index for a given lineage, with a hard ceiling $n_{\max}$. Let $\mathcal{K}_{\text{DNA}}$ represent a DNA integrity tensor and $\mathbf{J}_{\text{biochem}}$ a morphogen flux vector.

We define an invariant functional
\[
\mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}} = F\big(n, \mathcal{K}_{\text{DNA}}, \mathbf{J}_{\text{biochem}}\big),
\]
subject to the constraint
\[
\Delta \mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}} = 0.
\]
Any deviation $\Delta \mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}} \neq 0$ triggers an immediate corrective action by the suprakernel monitor, restoring $\mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}}$ to equilibrium.

\section{Tissue Manifold Closure $\mathcal{M}_{\text{close}}$}
We model tissue as a Riemannian manifold with metric tensor $g_{ij}$ and Ricci curvature tensor $R_{ij}$. Damage is represented as a metric distortion $\Psi_{\text{current}}^{(ij)}$ relative to a target homeostatic state $\Psi_{\text{target}}^{(ij)}$.

The closure engine applies an autonomous rate multiplier $\lambda_{\text{reg}}$ to drive convergence:
\[
g_{ij}(t + \Delta t) = g_{ij}(t) + \lambda_{\text{reg}} \cdot \big(\Psi_{\text{target}}^{(ij)} - \Psi_{\text{current}}^{(ij)}\big).
\]
Convergence is measured by a residual error
\[
\epsilon = \left\| \Psi_{\text{target}}^{(ij)} - \Psi_{\text{current}}^{(ij)} \right\|,
\]
with the goal $\epsilon \rightarrow 0$ (e.g., $\epsilon < 10^{-8}$) indicating zero-gap topological closure.

\section{Recursion-Safe Mitosis Model $R_{\text{mitosis}}$}
We treat mitosis as a recursion process with depth $n$. The recursion governor enforces:
\[
n \leq n_{\max}.
\]
If a forced request attempts $n > n_{\max}$, the governor:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Locks the lineage at $n_{\max}$.
  \item Halts further state-transfer operations.
  \item Transitions the lineage to terminal differentiation (NULL state).
\end{enumerate}
This mechanism mathematically prevents runaway growth and neoplastic drift.

\section{Sovereign Kernel Loop}
At each cycle, the Sovereign Regenerative Engine executes:

\subsection*{Phase 1: Sense \& Ingest}
Sample:
\[
\mathcal{E}_{\text{stress}}(t), \quad \mathcal{K}_{\text{DNA}}(t), \quad \Psi_{\text{current}}^{(ij)}(t)
\]
across $\Omega$.

\subsection*{Phase 2: Compute \& Validate}
Evaluate:
\[
T_{\text{reg}}(t), \quad \sigma_{\text{threshold}}(t), \quad \mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}}(t),
\]
and verify:
\[
\Delta \mathcal{I}_{\text{cc}} \equiv 0.
\]

\subsection*{Phase 3: Decide \& Act}
If conditions are safe:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Apply closure update to $g_{ij}$ via $\lambda_{\text{reg}}$.
  \item Permit mitosis only if $n < n_{\max}$ and $\mathcal{K}_{\text{DNA}}$ is nominal.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Phase 4: Stabilize \& Audit}
Re-assert invariant clamps, recompute $\epsilon$, and write all state transitions and invariant checks to a sovereign provenance ledger. The suprakernel maintains a \emph{sovereign lock} ensuring:
\[
\text{Topological invariants preserved}, \quad \text{neoplastic drift events} = 0.
\]

\section{Conclusion}
The Sovereign Regenerative Engine constitutes a unified, invariant-locked architecture for controlled biological regeneration. By combining timing control, strict cell-cycle invariants, geometric manifold closure, and recursion-safe mitosis, the system offers a principled path toward safe, whole-body regenerative technologies that avoid cancer, structural failure, and uncontrolled growth.

